# Intent: Hangman (id hangman, 30 English or 30 Spanish words compiled in for now, 26 keys, seven misses, a gallows drawn part by part, the word shown with spaces, a higher score for a longer word and fewer misses) and Mine Finder (id mines, 8 by 8, 10 mines, the first tap is never a mine, a flag switch on the right, the time in seconds is the score, lower is better) run on the unit through the grid engine. Each board is drawn by the one draw callback of the grid games. self.game.start, scores and list know both ids. Full quiet, the lock and updates refuse or end them as for every game; a game never moves the head.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal (ours)
# Check after a flash: self.game.start {name: "hangman"} shows the gallows, a blank word and 26 keys; tapping a key marks it green (in the word) or red (a miss) and adds a body part on a miss; self.game.start {name: "mines"} shows an 8 by 8 board; tapping a cell opens it (the first never a mine), the right-hand switch turns the taps into flags; both end with a banner and a score; a swipe down from the grabber ends the game.
# Kconfig: CONFIG_MAIPAI_GAMES (existing)
# Memory cost: about 4 KB of flash (measured: the plain image grew 4,144 bytes); a running game adds about 0.1 KB to the Grid struct in PSRAM and no LVGL object beyond the one board object (estimate for the internal cost: 600 bytes, as the other grid games).

